---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Navbar from '../../components/Navbar.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import DropZone from '../../components/DropZone.astro';
import BeforeAfter from '../../components/BeforeAfter.astro';
import RelatedPresets from '../../components/RelatedPresets.astro';
import MovieFaq from '../../components/MovieFaq.astro';
import { getPreset } from '../../lib/presets';

const preset = getPreset('fight-club');
const path = '/presets/fight-club/';

const title = 'Fight Club Photo Filter & Editor | Free Cinematic Tool';
const description =
	"Use this free Fight Club photo filter and photo editor to grade your photo with the film's sickly yellow-green, gritty 90s look, instantly in your browser — no account needed.";
const keywords =
	'fight club photo filter, fight club photo editor, fight club editor, fight club color grade, 90s photo filter';

const structuredData = {
	'@context': 'https://schema.org',
	'@type': 'WebPage',
	name: title,
	description,
	url: `https://cinematicphoto.com${path}`,
	isPartOf: { '@type': 'WebSite', name: 'Cinematic Photo', url: 'https://cinematicphoto.com' },
};

const faqs = [
	{
		q: 'Is this an official Fight Club photo filter?',
		a: "No. This preset is inspired by the visual aesthetic of Fight Club's cinematography, it isn't an official filter licensed by or affiliated with 20th Century Studios or the film's creators. It's an independent color grade built to echo the look, not a branded tie-in product.",
	},
	{
		q: 'Is there a dedicated Fight Club photo editor?',
		a: "This page is that dedicated editor — the Fight Club grade loads pre-selected in the drop zone above, so you can drop a photo and immediately adjust its grain, vignette, and glow without hunting through the full 60-preset library first.",
	},
	{
		q: 'Why does the Fight Club preset look sickly and desaturated instead of clean?',
		a: "That's intentional. Cinematographer Jeff Cronenweth pushed the film's color timing toward a deliberately unhealthy yellow-green, echoing the narrator's insomnia and the grimy, dilapidated spaces the story moves through. This preset chases that same off-kilter, faintly toxic cast rather than a punchy, saturated look.",
	},
	{
		q: 'How is Fight Club different from the Joker preset?',
		a: "Both lean into a sickly yellow-green palette, but the sources differ. Fight Club stays closer to a uniform, desaturated sepia-green wash across the whole frame, echoing its grungy late-90s underground setting. Joker splits its palette more directly between orange streetlight and green fluorescent, with heavier grain and a stronger warm-cool contrast.",
	},
	{
		q: 'Does the Fight Club preset work on daylight photos?',
		a: "Yes, though it's built around the film's dim, single-source interiors and industrial spaces, so it reads most convincingly on photos with some existing shadow or grime: basements, warehouses, dive bars, cluttered apartments. Bright, clean daylight shots still pick up the color shift, just less dramatically.",
	},
	{
		q: 'Why is the grain default so heavy on this preset?',
		a: "Fight Club leans into a grungy, unpolished texture that matches its themes of decay and rebellion against consumer polish. The preset's grain sits among the heaviest defaults in this tool for that reason, a clean, grain-free image would work against the whole aesthetic.",
	},
	{
		q: 'What kind of photos look best with a Fight Club-style color grade?',
		a: 'Industrial and run-down interiors, basements, warehouses, cluttered apartments, suit the grade best, since they already carry the grime and low light the palette is built to amplify. Moody single-source portraits also hold up well against the sickly yellow-green cast.',
	},
];
---

<Layout {title} {description} {keywords} {path} structuredData={structuredData}>
	<Navbar variant="landing" />
	<main>
		<Breadcrumbs
			items={[
				{ label: 'Home', href: '/' },
				{ label: 'All Presets', href: '/presets/' },
				{ label: 'Fight Club' },
			]}
		/>

		<DropZone presetId={preset.id} showHeading={false} showFooterLinks={false} />

		<section class="mx-auto flex w-full max-w-3xl flex-col items-center px-4 pb-4 text-center sm:px-6">
			<span class="mb-4 font-mono text-xs tracking-wide text-mute">MOVIE-INSPIRED PRESET</span>
			<h1 class="font-sans text-4xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-ink sm:text-5xl">
				The Fight Club photo filter: sickly yellow-green, gritty 90s.
			</h1>
			<p class="mt-5 max-w-xl text-base leading-7 text-body">
				A free Fight Club photo editor and color grading preset inspired by the visual aesthetic
				of David Fincher's Fight Club, built to give photos the film's grimy, unhealthy
				yellow-green cast without touching a curves panel.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl px-4 py-14 sm:px-6">
			<span class="mb-4 block font-mono text-xs tracking-wide text-mute">THE AESTHETIC</span>
			<h2 class="font-sans text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink sm:text-3xl">
				Grime as a color choice
			</h2>
			<div class="mt-6 flex flex-col gap-5 text-base leading-7 text-body">
				<p>
					Cinematographer Jeff Cronenweth, working closely with director David Fincher, built Fight
					Club's palette around a deliberately unhealthy color timing: desaturated, sepia-tinged
					yellow-greens that make even daylight scenes feel dim and slightly toxic. It's a look
					engineered to feel physically uncomfortable, matching the narrator's sleep-deprived,
					dissociated state of mind.
				</p>
				<p>
					That palette lives inside spaces that are themselves falling apart, condemned houses,
					dive bars, dingy basements, so the color grade and the production design reinforce each
					other. Grain runs heavy throughout, closer to a rough, degraded film stock than the
					clean, controlled cinematography of Fincher's later work.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl px-4 pb-14 sm:px-6">
			<span class="mb-4 block font-mono text-xs tracking-wide text-mute">BEFORE / AFTER</span>
			<h2 class="mb-6 font-sans text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink sm:text-3xl">
				See the grade applied
			</h2>
			<BeforeAfter preset={preset} />
		</section>

		<section class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl px-4 pb-14 sm:px-6">
			<span class="mb-4 block font-mono text-xs tracking-wide text-mute">THE COLOR SCIENCE</span>
			<h2 class="font-sans text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink sm:text-3xl">
				How the Fight Club color grade actually works
			</h2>
			<div class="mt-6 flex flex-col gap-5 text-base leading-7 text-body">
				<p>
					The base adjustment pushes contrast up while pulling saturation and brightness down, then
					layers in sepia to establish a dim, slightly jaundiced foundation before any directional
					color is added. A yellow-green overlay in overlay blend mode then sits across the whole
					frame, tying shadows and highlights together under the same sickly cast rather than
					splitting them in different directions.
				</p>
				<p>
					Grain runs among the heaviest in this tool's library, giving the image a rough, degraded
					texture that echoes the film's grungy 35mm look. Vignette stays moderate and glow is kept
					low, since the aesthetic is about grime and desaturation rather than soft, glowing bloom.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl px-4 pb-14 sm:px-6">
			<span class="mb-4 block font-mono text-xs tracking-wide text-mute">BEST PHOTOS FOR THIS PRESET</span>
			<h2 class="font-sans text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink sm:text-3xl">
				What to shoot it on
			</h2>
			<ul class="mt-6 flex flex-col gap-4 text-base leading-7 text-body">
				<li class="flex gap-3">
					<span class="mt-2 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-mute"></span>
					<span><span class="font-medium text-ink">Industrial or run-down interiors.</span> Basements, warehouses, and dive bars carry the grime and low light the grade is built to amplify.</span>
				</li>
				<li class="flex gap-3">
					<span class="mt-2 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-mute"></span>
					<span><span class="font-medium text-ink">Moody, single-source portraits.</span> A dim bulb or overhead light source matches the film's unglamorous character lighting.</span>
				</li>
				<li class="flex gap-3">
					<span class="mt-2 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-mute"></span>
					<span><span class="font-medium text-ink">Cluttered, lived-in spaces.</span> Texture and mess read as intentional grime under this grade rather than visual noise.</span>
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<RelatedPresets
			heading="More movie-inspired grades"
			items={[
				{ presetId: 'seven', href: '/?preset=seven' },
				{ presetId: 'silence-of-the-lambs', href: '/?preset=silence-of-the-lambs' },
				{ presetId: 'shutter-island', href: '/?preset=shutter-island' },
				{ presetId: 'joker', href: '/presets/joker/' },
				{ presetId: 'memento', href: '/?preset=memento' },
			]}
		/>

		<MovieFaq
			eyebrow="FIGHT CLUB FAQ"
			heading="Common questions about the Fight Club photo filter"
			faqs={faqs}
		/>

		<section class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl px-4 py-14 text-center sm:px-6">
			<p class="text-base leading-7 text-body">
				Prefer a different film's look? Browse the <a href="/presets/" class="text-ink underline decoration-hairline-strong underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-ink">full library of 60 presets</a>,
				see more <a href="/looks/dark-and-moody/" class="text-ink underline decoration-hairline-strong underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-ink">dark and moody presets</a>,
				or head to the <a href="/" class="text-ink underline decoration-hairline-strong underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-ink">homepage</a> to start with a blank slate.
			</p>
		</section>
	</main>
	<Footer />
</Layout>

<script>
	import '../../lib/landing';
</script>
